#include <iostream>
#include <stop_token>
#include <thread>

int main() {
    std::jthread t([](std::stop_token st) {
        while (!st.stop_requested()) {
            // poll the book, poll the queue, ... C++17 equivalent needs a
            // hand-rolled std::atomic<bool> flag checked in the loop
        }
        std::cout << "stopped cleanly\n";
    });
    t.request_stop();   // the destructor would request stop and join anyway
    return 0;
}

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

int counter = 0;                     // shared, unprotected: a data race
void bump(int n) { for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) ++counter; }

int main() {
    std::thread a(bump, 100000), b(bump, 100000);
    a.join();
    b.join();
    std::cout << counter << '\n';    // often below 200000, never guaranteed
    return 0;
}

guidedExercise.solution:
```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

void sumQuarter(const std::vector<double>& v, int q, std::vector<double>& out) {
    std::size_t begin = q * v.size() / 4, end = (q + 1) * v.size() / 4;
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = begin; i < end; ++i) s += v[i];
    out[q] = s;                      // distinct element per thread: no race
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> data(1'000'000, 1.0);
    std::vector<double> partial(4, 0.0);
    std::vector<std::thread> ts;
    for (int q = 0; q < 4; ++q)
        ts.emplace_back(sumQuarter, std::cref(data), q, std::ref(partial));
    for (auto& t : ts) t.join();     // happens-before: worker writes -> main reads
    double total = 0;
    for (double p : partial) total += p;
    std::cout << total << '\n';      // 1000000, deterministically
}
```
The four writes hit four distinct elements, so there is no conflict between workers. The vector `data` is never written after the threads start (immutable once shared). The final reads of `partial` are ordered after the workers' writes by `join()`, which gives a happens-before edge, without the joins, reading `partial` would itself be a race. A single shared `total += s` inside each worker would be a data race on one location; lessons 2 and 4 show the mutex and atomic fixes.

independentExercise.solution:
**Bug 1, terminate at scope exit:** the thread is still joinable when the function returns; destroying a joinable `std::thread` calls `std::terminate()`, aborting the process. **Bug 2, data race on `buf`:** `buf[0]` is read while `load_prices` may be writing it, with no synchronisation ordering the accesses, undefined behaviour, so the program may print garbage or anything else. Fix:```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

std::vector<double> buf;

void load_prices(std::vector<double>& b) { b.push_back(42.0); }

// C++17 fix: join before reading; consider a scope guard for exception safety
void run() {
    std::thread t(load_prices, std::ref(buf));
    t.join();                        // orders loader's writes before main's read
    std::cout << buf[0] << '\n';
}

// C++20 fix: jthread joins on destruction, so scope exit is safe
// void run20() {
//     std::jthread t(load_prices, std::ref(buf));
//     t.join();                        // still join explicitly before reading
//     std::cout << buf[0] << '\n';
// }
```
Note that `std::jthread` alone fixes only bug 1; the read of `buf` still needs the `join()` (or other synchronisation) before it. A destructor that joins is a lifecycle fix, not a data-race fix.

